{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon ECR introduces archive storage class for rarely accessed container images - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) has introduced a new archive storage class to help reduce storage costs for infrequently accessed container images. This new feature allows users to meet compliance and retention requirements while optimizing storage expenses. ECR's lifecycle policies now support archiving images based on last pull time, enabling automatic archiving based on usage patterns. Users can also archive images individually using the ECR Console or API. Archived images do not count towards the image per repository limit and are inaccessible for pulls but can be easily restored within 20 minutes via the ECR Console, CLI, or API. All archival and restore operations are logged through CloudTrail for auditability. The new ECR archive storage class is available in all AWS Commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For pricing details, visit the AWS pricing page. To learn more, consult the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-ecr-archive-storage-class-container-images/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"EC2 Image Builder enhances lifecycle policies with wildcard support and simplified IAM - AWS","context":"EC2 Image Builder, a service offered by AWS for automating the creation, distribution, and management of customized Amazon Machine Images, has introduced two new features to simplify image management. Firstly, wildcard patterns are now supported in lifecycle policies, allowing users to manage images from multiple recipes within a single policy. This feature automatically applies lifecycle policies to all matching recipes, including new ones created in the future. Secondly, IAM role creation for lifecycle management has been simplified, with the console now automatically populating the required default permissions when creating a new role. Previously, users had to create separate lifecycle policies for each new recipe or manually configure permissions, making it difficult to manage image lifecycles at scale. These enhancements reduce setup time and potential configuration errors, enabling users to manage their image lifecycle more efficiently. Lifecycle Policies are available in all commercial AWS regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/ec2-image-builder-lifecycle-enhancements/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"processing-s3-uploads-with-step-functions","skill_description":"Deploy an event-driven workflow that routes S3 uploads to either Lambda or Fargate via Step Functions based on file size. Uses EventBridge to trigger a Step Functions state machine when objects are uploaded to S3. Small files are processed by Lambda, large files by a Fargate task. Includes VPC, ECR repository, ECS cluster, and scoped IAM roles. Trigger keywords: Step Functions, Fargate, Lambda, S3 event, EventBridge, ECS, ECR, file processing, workflow orchestration, serverless.\n\n\nServices: step-functions, lambda, s3, eventbridge, ecs, ecr, fargate\nTasks: deploy, configure\nPersona: backend-developer\nWorkload: serverless, containers\n*","skill_name":"processing-s3-uploads-with-step-functions"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS Config now supports 191 additional managed rules - AWS","context":"AWS Config has added support for 191 new managed rules across various services including Amazon Bedrock, Amazon SageMaker, Amazon ECS, Amazon EKS, Amazon RDS, Amazon Redshift, Amazon S3, and Amazon CloudTrail. These rules cover encryption, logging, public access, network security, data protection, and operational best practices. Users can deploy these rules individually or as part of a conformance pack. The new rules include ACM_CERTIFICATE_RSA_CHECK for AWS Certificate Manager, API_GWV2_ACCESS_LOGS_ENABLED for Amazon API Gateway, APPSYNC_AUTHORIZATION_CHECK and APPSYNC_LOGGING_ENABLED for AWS AppSync, and many more for other services. The expansion increases built-in governance coverage across AI workloads and core cloud infrastructure","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-config-additional-managed-rules/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-serverless","skill_description":"Routes serverless requests to a compute form factor and the skill that owns it. Applies first to Lambda, API Gateway, EventBridge, Step Functions and event-driven work while the compute, orchestration or integration choice is open — API backends, webhooks, jobs, cron, queue/stream consumers, durable workflows, event routing, Step Functions vs Durable Functions, and Pipes vs native event source mappings for queues and streams. Decides Event Functions on-demand vs Managed Instances, Web Functions, MicroVMs, EventBridge rules/Scheduler/Pipes/bus, HTTP vs REST API. Owns shared Lambda references (cold starts, SnapStart, concurrency, event sources, function URLs, throttling, troubleshooting). Defers to the specialized skill once a request commits to one form factor or service, or describes its defining feature, even unnamed (durable checkpointing and automatic step retries, Firecracker sandbox, managed instances, Node.js server). Not for EC2, ECS/EKS/Fargate or Amplify hosting.\n\nServices: lambda, api-gateway, step-functions, eventbridge, dynamodb, sqs, sns, s3, kinesis\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, optimize, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: serverless\n*","skill_name":"aws-serverless"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify how ECR lifecycle policy rule priority and image counting evaluation works", "parent_id": "60bdd6d5-b647-4882-b64b-bacb5d48f343"}
